# What must never enter a build context.
#
# The first reason is correctness: `node_modules` holds compiled native
# binaries for whichever machine installed them. Copied from a developer's Mac
# into a Linux image, `pnpm install` then considers the tree satisfied and the
# build fails looking for `lightningcss.linux-arm64-gnu.node`. CI never hits
# this — a fresh checkout has no `node_modules` — which is exactly what makes
# it the kind of bug that only ever appears on someone's laptop.
#
# The second is size. The context is uploaded to the builder before anything
# runs, and `.git` plus `node_modules` is most of a gigabyte of it.

node_modules
**/node_modules

.git
.gitignore

# Build output, rebuilt inside the image.
.next
**/.next
dist
**/dist
**/*.tsbuildinfo
.turbo
**/.turbo

# Python, in case the root context ever sees it.
.venv
**/.venv
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
*.pyc

# Secrets and local state. None of this belongs in a layer.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
backups
**/generated/realm-prod.json

# Third-party data fetched at runtime, 124 MB of it.
backend/data

.DS_Store
*.log
